AB  - Evapotranspiration (ET) datasets derived from satellites, hydrological models, and reanalyses each have distinct strengths and limitations. Relying on a single source can bias interpretations. To address this, we present evapoRe, an R package for efficient access, visualization, and analysis of ET and potential evapotranspiration (PET) across spatial and temporal scales. evapoRe includes 22 homogenized datasets (18 ET, 4 PET) and six supporting datasets for PET estimations, covering 1948–2023 at 0.25° resolution and daily to annual scales. It also includes 12 PET estimation methods, from empirical to physically based. We demonstrate evapoRe’s capabilities through: (i) global evaluation with FLUXNET, (ii) regional analyses over the Mediterranean, (iii) case studies in Spain for drought and extremes, and (iv) an Iberian Peninsula assessment for ET product benchmarking. By unifying datasets and methods, evapoRe offers a flexible, accessible tool to support diverse research on land–atmosphere interactions and enable robust ET and PET analysis.
