AB  - Parameter calibration for urban fluvial flood models is challenging due to model complexity and computational budget limitations. To address this issue, this study proposes a novel parameter calibration framework that integrates urban flood models with widely used building treatment methods and a classifier-assisted level-based learning swarm optimizer. The stability and independence of the calibrated parameters were evaluated through laboratory experiments, and the proposed framework successfully reduced the RMSE by 23.75% and 19.92% for the building-resistance and building-porosity methods, respectively. The applicability of different building treatment methods from a parameter calibration perspective was also discussed. The proposed framework was then applied to a real-world flood case, where the RMSE values of three cross-validation combinations using flood mark data were 0.4464, 0.3690, and 0.3992, respectively, demonstrating its generalization ability. Furthermore, under the same computational budget, the proposed framework achieved higher computational efficiency compared with traditional evolutionary algorithms.
